use console.println

/// Writes `x` with `digits` places. Not the standard library: an ordinary
/// Cove function with a caller, between `main` and the operation that
/// refuses, so that the diagnostic below has a frame to blame that is neither
/// the entry point nor the machine.
fn written(x: Float, digits: Int) -> String {
  x.format(digits)
}

/// Pins `Float.format`'s refusal of a digit count past seventeen, word for
/// word.
///
/// A `Float` carries at most 17 significant decimal digits, so an eighteenth
/// asks for padding rather than for precision and the operation refuses it.
/// `std.float`'s `digitsRefused` and `digitsRule` build the two sentences
/// now, once, in Cove. They were `format_digits` in the VM's `operand.rs`
/// and `format_digits_error` in the interpreter's `builtins.rs`, two copies in
/// Rust, and this file is what held the two in agreement and then held the
/// move to their words.
///
/// This is the half of `Float.format`'s contract that
/// `tests/e2e/values_float_format` cannot hold, because a refusal stops the
/// run and takes every row after it: one case, one refusal.
/// [ADR 0064](../../../docs/adr/0064-an-intrinsic-names-a-machine-not-a-method.md)'s
/// Decision 8 gates a migration on the diagnostic being unchanged, and this
/// file is where "unchanged" is written down.
///
/// **It said `Float.format` could not move, until it could.** Decision 2
/// refuses a primitive "whose policy is a branch on its argument", and names
/// `format`'s 17 digits as one of the three examples — so the branch belongs
/// in Cove, and a Cove body could not raise
/// ([issue #461](https://github.com/myuon/cove/issues/461)). ADR 0067's
/// `core.refuse` is what it raises with now: the message, the rule and the
/// blamed line are the ones below, and the one thing the move added is the
/// `std/float.cove` context block ADR 0058's rule asks for, as `Int.abs` and
/// `Int.parseRadix` show.
///
/// `Float.parse` is **not**, which is the distinction worth reading these two
/// files together for: every one of its refusals is an `Err` value carrying a
/// message that quotes only its own argument, so there is nothing in
/// `tests/e2e/values_float_parse` a Cove body could not build.
export fn main() -> Result<Unit, Error> {
  println(written(1.5, 18))?
  Ok(())
}
